  "history": "Thursday is Thor's day. Thor, or Thunor in Old English, was the god of thunder, and his name is simply the old word for thunder. The Anglo-Saxons said Þunresdæg, ‘Thunor's day’. Later the English form was probably changed by the Old Norse name Þórsdagr, and Thursday came from that. The Romans gave the day to Jupiter, the king of the gods, who threw lightning: dies Iovis, today French jeudi and Spanish jueves. The Germanic peoples chose their own thunder god for it. German Donnerstag and Dutch donderdag show the old link even more clearly: Donner and donder mean ‘thunder’.",
  "historyEinfach": "Thursday means ‘Thor's day’. Thor was the god of thunder. Thunder is the loud noise in a storm. The Romans gave this day to their god Jupiter. He also made storms. In German, the day is Donnerstag. Donner means thunder.",
